"lemma":"வந்த","gloss":"that came"}],"gloss":"that came"},{"surface":"வைகல்","parts":[{"text":"வைகல்","lemma":"வைகல்","gloss":"day"}],"gloss":"day"},{"surface":"அல்லது","parts":[{"text":"அல்லது","lemma":"அல்லது","gloss":"except"}],"gloss":"except"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"சென்ற","parts":[{"text":"சென்ற","lemma":"சென்ற","gloss":"that went"}],"gloss":"that went"},{"surface":"எல்லை","parts":[{"text":"எல்லை","lemma":"எல்லை","gloss":"day"}],"gloss":"day"},{"surface":"செலவு","parts":[{"text":"செலவு","lemma":"செலவு","gloss":"going"}],"gloss":"going"},{"surface":"அறி","parts":[{"text":"அறி","lemma":"அறி","gloss":"know"}],"gloss":"know"},{"surface":"யேனே","parts":[{"text":"யேனே","lemma":"ஏன்","gloss":"I do not"}],"gloss":"I do not"}]}],"literal":"In the soft-soil region, together resting / on the branch of a Vanci tree, the sleeping heron / eats the flowers of the cut sugarcane, / while in the hard-soil region, the black-stemmed millet's / ... / beautiful-eyed small hares are scared, and nearby / the black-branched mahua flowers drip down. / Even if there is no festival, the farmers' bowls / are filled with big keṭiṟu fish food and flower-strewn toddy. / ... we of the kiṇai drum, O Lord! / Paddy is nothing to us, gold is nothing to us. / The glowing, hot toddy called / ... house is nothing to us, and many such things, / even when I desisted, he did not desist from giving. / Amidst the fat-rich, abundant rice, / making the earth feel ashamed, desiring fame, / making water feel ashamed, distributing ghee, / he protected us, our father. How can we perish? / We have such a patron, they say. Hereafter, to poverty, / let the star Venus stand anywhere! Glorious / good vessels from which we ate, we place and shake, / except for the days we came to him, / I do not know of any day we went away empty-handed.","translation":"The heron that sleeps resting on the branch of a vañci tree in the soft-soil region feeds on the flowers of the cut sugarcane. In the hard-soil region, the black-stemmed millet grows, where beautiful-eyed small hares are startled, and nearby, flowers drip from the black-branched mahua trees. Even when there is no festival, the bowls of the farmers are filled with large keṭiṟu fish and flower-strewn toddy. We are players of the kiṇai drum, O Lord! Paddy is nothing to us, gold is nothing to us, nor is the glowing, hot toddy, nor are houses; though I desisted from asking, he did not desist from giving all these. Desiring fame that puts the earth to shame, and distributing ghee so generously that water itself would feel ashamed, our father protected us. How can we perish? We can proudly say we have such a patron. Hereafter, let the glorious star Venus stand in any direction! We only know the days we came to him, shaking the fine vessels from which we ate; we know of no day we went away from him.","modern":"மென்மையான மருத நிலப் பகுதியில், வஞ்சி மரத்தின் கிளையில் தங்கி உறங்கும் நாரையானது, வெட்டப்பட்ட கரும்பின் பூக்களை உண்ணும். வன்மையான முல்லை நிலப் பகுதியில், கரிய தண்டுடைய வரகுப் பயிர்கள் விளையும் இடத்தில், அழகிய கண்களைக் கொண்ட முயல்கள் அஞ்சியோட, அருகில் உள்ள கரிய கிளைகளைக் கொண்ட இருப்பை மரத்தின் பூக்கள் உதிரும். விழாக்கள் இல்லாத நாட்களிலும், உழவர்களின் பாத்திரங்களில் பெரிய கெடிற்று மீன் உணவும் பூக்கள் கலந்த கள்ளும் நிறைந்திருக்கும். கிணைப் பறையை முழக்கும் சுற்றத்தாராகிய எமக்கு, நெல்லும் பொன்னும் ஒரு பொருட்டல்ல; சுடச்சுடத் தரும் கள்ளும் வீடும் கூட எமக்கு வேண்டாம் என்று நான் தடுத்தாலும், அவன் தட்டாமல் பலவற்றையும் வாரி வழங்கினான். உலகமே நாணும்படி புகழை விரும்பி, நீர் நாணும்படி நெய்யை வாரி வழங்கி எங்களைக் காத்த தந்தை அவன். நாம் எவ்வாறு வறுமையால் அழிவோம்? அப்படிப்பட்ட வள்ளலை நாம் உடையோம் என்று கூறுவோம். இனி வறுமை எங்களை அணுகாது. மழை தரும் வெள்ளித் திசை எங்கு நின்றாலும் எமக்குக் கவலையிலலை. அவன் தந்த நல்ல பாத்திரங்களில் உண்டு மகிழ்ந்து, அவற்றை அசைத்துக் கழுவும் நாட்களைத் தவிர, அவனிடமிருந்து வறுமையோடு சென்ற நாட்களை யாம் அறியோம்.","explanation":"This poem is a 'kaiyaṟunilai' (elegy) sung by the poet Puṟattiṇai Naṉṉākaṉār on the death of his patron, Karumpaṉūr Kiḻāṉ, under the 'pāṭāṇ' tiṇai. The poet recalls the immense generosity of the chieftain, who ruled over a fertile land where both soft agricultural tracts (marutam) and hard forest tracts (mullai) flourished. He describes how the chieftain would relentlessly shower the bards with gifts of rice, ghee, gold, and houses, even when they desisted from asking. The poet proudly declares that as long as they had such a patron, they never had to worry about droughts or the position of the star Venus (which indicates rainfall). Now, in his absence, the poet laments their loss, remembering only the days of abundance they shared with him.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"},"timing":{"male":[[0.0,0.5],[0.5,0.8],[0.8,1.22],[1.22,1.5],[1.5,1.62],[1.62,2.1],[2.1,2.4],[2.4,2.8],[2.8,3.0],[3.0,3.42],[3.42,3.86],[3.86,3.95],[3.95,4.38],[4.38,4.56],[4.56,4.82],[4.82,5.26],[5.26,5.61],[5.61,5.96],[5.96,6.4],[6.4,6.66],[6.66,7.31],[7.31,8.6],[8.6,9.67],[9.67,10.53],[10.53,11.18],[11.18,12.04],[12.04,12.9],[12.9,13.8],[13.8,14.0],[14.0,15.1],[15.1,15.8],[15.8,16.2],[16.2,17.05],[17.05,17.9],[17.9,18.1],[18.1,19.1],[19.1,19.4],[19.4,19.9],[19.9,20.2],[20.2,20.96],[20.96,21.5],[21.5,21.8],[21.8,22.1],[22.1,22.7],[22.7,23.12],[23.12,23.4],[23.4,23.7],[23.7,24.6],[24.6,25.1],[25.1,25.3],[25.3,25.6],[25.6,26.0],[26.4,27.0],[27.0,27.47],[27.47,27.7],[27.7,28.4],[28.4,28.7],[28.7,28.9],[28.9,29.2],[29.2,30.2],[30.2,30.4],[30.4,30.6],[30.6,31.1],[31.72,32.32],[32.32,32.82],[32.82,33.02],[33.02,33.22],[33.22,33.72],[33.72,34.32],[34.32,34.82],[34.82,35.02],[35.02,35.29],[35.29,35.82],[35.82,36.32],[36.32,36.82],[36.82,37.22],[37.22,37.72],[37.72,38.25],[38.25,38.39],[38.39,38.7],[38.7,39.32],[39.32,39.9],[39.9,40.62],[40.62,41.12],[41.12,41.62],[41.62,42.0],[42.0,42.32],[42.32,42.58],[42.58,42.92]],"female":[[0.0,0.5],[0.5,0.8],[0.8,1.3],[1.3,1.6],[1.6,1.72],[1.72,2.2],[2.2,2.5],[2.5,2.9],[2.9,3.38],[3.38,4.1],[4.1,4.5],[4.5,4.8],[4.8,5.3],[5.3,6.0],[6.0,6.3],[6.3,6.6],[6.6,7.4],[7.